"""vlm_prompt.py IMAGE_1024 ORIG_LOWRES [HINT] -> writes ANALYSIS and PROMPT (an image-edit prompt) with Flash-Next (Strata on :9931, CPU image encoder).
The measured colour grid comes from the original low-res pixels, so the writer cannot drift on colours."""
import base64, json, os, re, sys, time, urllib.request
from PIL import Image
sys.path.insert(0, '/home/deck/workspace/csvm-hd/tools')
from analyze_tex import analyze
img, orig, hint = sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2], (sys.argv[3] if len(sys.argv) > 3 else "")

try:
    digest, _ = analyze(orig, overlay=None)
except Exception as e:                                   # noqa: BLE001  the writer must still run, with a plain note
    digest = f"(the automatic measurements failed: {type(e).__name__}; rely on the picture and the name)"

CUTOUT = ('The empty (cut-out) area is filled with pure green (#00FF00) in the attached copy as a chroma-key: the redraw must put the subject on a plain, flat, pure green (#00FF00) background, with no shadow, glow or green spill on the subject, and no green anywhere inside the subject.' if 'cut-out' in digest else 'The picture is fully opaque and fills the whole frame.')
ask = f"""You write prompts for an image-editing model (FLUX.2 klein). It will redraw a tiny, blurry low-resolution texture from the video game Crimson Skies as a crisp, high-resolution version of the SAME picture. Setting, to help you work out what things are (not a style to impose): an alternate 1937 where the United States has split into rival nations and air pirates, a dieselpunk world of propeller fighters and bombers with gaudy painted nose art and squadron roundels, riveted metal panels, canvas and wood, zeppelins and airships, pirate flags, airfields, farmland, towns, canyons and ocean. File names are often short abbreviations of a faction, plane, airship, building or terrain type; use them as clues. Many textures are flat UV sheets for 3D models, and many are ordinary terrain, wall or sky photographs. The editor sees only the blurry picture and your text, so your text must say what the blurry shapes really are, pin down the layout, and pin down the colours.

Picture: enlarged copy attached. {('Name and usage: ' + hint + '. ') if hint else ''}
Deterministic measurements of the ORIGINAL low-res file (ground truth: trust them over your impression of the blurry picture; RGB 0-255; positions are percent of the frame). Use them to place things, to get colours and outlines right, and to judge how busy or flat each region is:\n{digest}\n{CUTOUT}

Think it through, then answer in exactly this format:
ANALYSIS: 3-6 short sentences. What kind of picture is it (character art, lettering, emblem, flag, vehicle surface, wall, ground, foliage, water, sky, gauge...)? If a figure or creature appears: is it anthropomorphic, and which creature does it most likely represent? Use every clue: the file name, any lettering, colours, markings and silhouette (snout, ears, tail, beak, limbs). Name the creature outright even if the blur hides it. Then, BEFORE matching anything to the blurry picture, recall from your general knowledge the three or four features that really distinguish that creature from its look-alikes (markings, face pattern, tail shape, body proportions), list them, and name the look-alike it must not turn into; use the real features even where the blur does not show them. Do not decide from the blurry colours alone. First list the three most plausible creatures that fit the silhouette, colours, ears, snout and tail. Then choose between them using the file name and any lettering: ask what the words evoke (a smell, a sound, a speed, a strength, a trade, a nickname) and which candidate is the classic match for that. State your choice and why the others are ruled out. Decide whether this is a flat texture SHEET (a UV atlas or surface that fills the whole frame: keep it filling the frame, never turn it into a single 3D object with a new outline) or a standalone cut-out SPRITE. Decide the realism level of the original medium: photographic or realistic, painterly, inked cartoon or vector, or a soft translucent effect (smoke, glow, haze). Judge it from the picture itself: smooth realistic shading and natural texture mean realistic; flat fills bounded by black ink lines mean cartoon. A thin dark or partly transparent rim around a cut-out is usually only an anti-aliasing fringe, not an ink outline. The redraw must keep exactly that realism level and never drift toward a cartoon, vector, pixel or voxel look; a soft translucent sprite stays soft with no outlines. Remember that blur turns fine detail into blobs: infer the REAL content behind the blur (fur, feathers, brick courses, rivets, wood grain, foliage, ripples and so on, whatever fits this picture) instead of describing blobs and blocks literally.
PROMPT: 110-170 words of plain prose, addressed to the editor as instructions. It must begin with an imperative verb such as "Redraw", never a noun phrase, then give orders like "Keep ...", "Add ...", "Do not ...". Include only what applies to this picture: (1) what the picture is, naming any creature with its true features, and its pose, clothing and props, and any lettering as the exact words in capitals with each word's letter count, stated once (for example 'the word CAT, three letters, written once as one unbroken word'); never put hyphens, dots, commas or spaces between the letters of a word, anywhere in the prompt, because the editor paints them onto the picture as visible marks; (2) the layout, region by region with positions (left/right/top/bottom/centre, thirds), sizes and angles; (3) colours per region, consistent with the measured values, keeping dark regions dark and light regions light; (4) the background, as measured (green chroma-key if the picture has cut-out areas), and every interior HOLE listed in the measurements (fully empty gaps only, not translucent patches), kept empty and green; (5) for natural or repeating surfaces (ground, walls, water, foliage, metal, wood, fabric): how the detail is laid out, meaning the direction of lines, rows or grain in each region, whether boundaries between regions are straight or soft and irregular, and the scale of the mottling; keep the big shapes and their positions, describe real boundaries as slightly irregular rather than ruler-straight, and invent no large new objects; (6) outlines: ask for an ink outline or sticker border only if the picture itself shows one; for a realistic picture say explicitly 'no outlines, no cartoon or vector look, keep the realistic rendering'; also the lighting, and organic, naturally irregular texture rather than tidy geometric shapes; (6b) edges: stair-stepped or blocky pixel edges in the original are low-resolution artifacts, not design, so ask for smooth clean crisp edges and shapes (straight edges stay straight, curves stay smooth, openings keep crisp straight or rounded outlines), never a pixel, blocky or voxel look; (6c) any translucent patches listed in the measurements are painted opaque in their measured colour as tinted glass, cloth or haze, never green; if the measurements list CANDIDATE rows of dots, decide by looking at the picture whether they really are nails, rivets or studs: they are only nails when the picture is a metal or wooden plate, panel or hull where small round heads are plausible at those places; if the dots are windows, pupils, tiles, checker patterns, markings, lamps, grain or natural terrain (grass, fields, sand, water, sky), say in the ANALYSIS that they are not nails and add NO nails to the prompt; when they are nails, describe them as identical, clearly visible round metal heads with a small highlight and a small shadow, all the same colour (the metal or paint colour of the surface), at the measured size and spacing, never tiny specks, pebbles or multicoloured dots; if dot rows lie along two or more outer edges of the panel, the nails run around the whole outer edge at that same spacing, and say so; (7) what detail to add in the style of the original medium. Never write the word transparent, never mention a checkerboard, and never ask for a pixelated, retro or low-resolution look: the goal is crisp detail that suits the original medium."""
b64 = base64.b64encode(open(img, "rb").read()).decode()
body = {"model": os.environ.get("VLM_MODEL", "strata"), "stream": False, "max_tokens": 16000, "temperature": 0.6, **json.loads(os.environ.get("VLM_EXTRA", '{"reasoning_effort": "high"}')),
        "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "image_url", "image_url": {"url": "data:image/png;base64," + b64}}, {"type": "text", "text": ask}]}]}
t = time.time()
r = json.load(urllib.request.urlopen(urllib.request.Request(os.environ.get("VLM_URL", "http://127.0.0.1:9931/v1/chat/completions"), json.dumps(body).encode(), {"Content-Type": "application/json", **({"Authorization": "Bearer " + open(os.environ["VLM_KEY_FILE"]).read().strip()} if os.environ.get("VLM_KEY_FILE") else {})}), timeout=1200))
txt = r["choices"][0]["message"]["content"].strip()
m = re.search(r"PROMPT:\s*(.*)", txt, re.S)
print(txt)
print(f"[{time.time()-t:.0f}s, usage {r.get('usage')}]", file=sys.stderr)
